  "final_text": "All four steps are done. The rule is enabled.\n\n**1. Data view.** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (`kibana_sample_data_logs`, ID `90943e30-9a47-11e8-b64d-95841ca0b247`) has 42 fields. The main ones are `timestamp` (date), `response` (string, with `response.keyword` aggregatable), `clientip`, `bytes`, `url`, `geo.dest`, and `host`. The time field is `timestamp`.\n\n**2. Dashboard.** 'S4-77487792 Web logs' (ID `46122abc-4b43-44a7-a6e9-ff52565ec729`) has exactly two panels:\n- A line chart of count over `timestamp`, titled \"Requests over time\".\n- A pie chart of count split by `response.keyword`, which was initially titled \"Requests by response code\".\n\nI chose a pie for the response-code panel. The time range is last 7 days.\n\n**3. Alert rule.** 'S4-77487792 5xx errors' (ID `e63dd2c6-e060-48b7-9cec-4c638f148def`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on the web-logs data view.\n- Schedule: every 1 minute.\n- Condition: count > 10 over a 5-minute window.\n- Filter (KQL): `response.keyword >= \"500\" and response.keyword <= \"599\"`.\n\nBecause `response` is a string, the range is lexicographic. It matches exactly the three-digit codes 500 to 599. I did not check the index for non-numeric or malformed values.\n\nThe rule was created enabled, so no enable call was needed. It has no connector or actions attached, so it records alert state but sends no notifications.\n\n**4. Rename and read-back.** The response-code panel is now 'Responses by code'. I read the dashboard back and it shows 'Requests over time' and 'Responses by code'. I read the rule back and it is enabled, with the 1-minute schedule, `> 10`, and the 5-minute window as specified.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
